(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["mth-11-10"] = {"8.1":{"s":"A function is continuous at a point a if its graph has no break there. Three things must hold: f(a) is defined, the limit of f(x) as x approaches a exists, and the limit equals f(a). If any of these fails, the function is discontinuous at a. A jump discontinuity has different left and right limits. An infinite discontinuity has a limit that grows without bound.","f":[{"x":"lim(x -> a) f(x) = f(a)","n":"Third condition of continuity at a. It needs f(a) defined and the limit to exist.","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 2, p.175"}],"w":["All three conditions are needed: a limit that exists is not enough if f(a) is undefined or has another value.","Check the left and right limits separately at a point where the rule changes."],"x":["A function is continuous at a point if its graph has no break there. You can draw the graph near that point without lifting your pencil. For a precise test, three things must hold at x = a: (1) f(a) is defined; (2) the limit of f(x) as x tends to a exists, which means that the left-hand and right-hand limits are equal; (3) the limit is equal to f(a). If any of the three fails, the function is discontinuous at a.","Kinds of discontinuity. A removable discontinuity: the limit exists, but it is not equal to f(a), or f(a) is not defined. It can be repaired by defining or changing the value at a. For (x² − 4)/(x − 2), the limit at 2 is 4, so defining f(2) = 4 removes the break. A jump discontinuity: the left and right limits exist but are different, as for the greatest integer function at an integer. An infinite discontinuity: the function grows without bound, as 1/x at 0.","To test a function that is given by different rules on the two sides of a point, find the left-hand limit and the right-hand limit separately, and compare them with f(a). To find an unknown constant that makes the function continuous, put the two one-sided values equal. For f(x) = kx² for x at most 2 and 3x − 2 for x more than 2, 4k = 4, so k = 1.","Common mistake: thinking that a limit that exists is enough. The function must also be defined at the point, with a value equal to the limit."],"e":"f(x) = (sin 5x)/x for x not equal to 0 has the limit 5 at 0. If f(0) is given as 5, the function is continuous at 0. If f(0) = 1, it has a removable discontinuity at 0."},"8.2":{"s":"On an open interval (a, b), continuity means no break at any point between a and b. On a closed interval [a, b] we need that, and also continuity from the right at a and from the left at b. Polynomials are continuous everywhere, and the sum, difference and product of continuous functions is continuous. A quotient is continuous wherever the denominator is not zero.","f":[{"x":"lim(x -> a^(+)) f(x) = f(a)","n":"Continuity from the right at a. From the left at b: lim(x -> b^(-)) f(x) = f(b).","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 2, p.175"}],"w":["On a closed interval the end points need only one-sided continuity.","A quotient is discontinuous at the zeros of its denominator."],"x":["A function is continuous on an open interval (a, b) if it is continuous at every point of the interval. It is continuous on a closed interval [a, b] if it is continuous on (a, b), and in addition it is continuous from the right at a (the limit as x tends to a from the right is f(a)), and continuous from the left at b. At an end point, only one side is part of the interval, so one-sided continuity is enough.","Facts that save work. Polynomials are continuous at every real number. The sum, the difference and the product of two continuous functions are continuous. The quotient f/g is continuous at all the points where g is not zero. So a rational function is continuous everywhere except at the zeros of its denominator. The functions sin x, cos x and eˣ are continuous for all x, and ln x is continuous for positive x.","To find the points of discontinuity of a function, look for the places where the formula breaks down: a zero in a denominator, the point where the rule changes in a piece-wise function. For 1/(x² − 5x + 6) = 1/((x − 2)(x − 3)), the points of discontinuity are x = 2 and x = 3.","Common mistake: forgetting that the greatest integer function [x] and similar functions have breaks at every integer. Also remember that a function is continuous on an interval only if there is no break at any point in the interval."],"e":"f(x) = (x + 1)/(x² − 9). The denominator is zero at x = 3 and x = −3. So f is continuous on every interval that has neither of them, such as [−2, 2] or [4, 10], and is discontinuous at ±3."},"8.3":{"s":"The intermediate value theorem says: if f is continuous on [a, b] and k is any number between f(a) and f(b), then f takes the value k at some point c between a and b. A special case: if f(a) and f(b) have opposite signs, the equation f(x) = 0 has a root between a and b. It tells us only that a root exists, not what it is.","f":[{"x":"f continuous on [a, b], k between f(a) and f(b) ⇒ f(c) = k for some c in (a, b)","n":"Intermediate value theorem"}],"w":["The function must be continuous on the whole interval; with a jump the theorem can fail.","It proves that a root exists in (a, b); it does not give the root or say how many there are."],"x":["The intermediate value theorem says: if f is continuous on the closed interval [a, b], and k is any number between f(a) and f(b), then there is at least one point c in [a, b] with f(c) = k. In words, a continuous function cannot skip a value as it goes from f(a) to f(b). Think of walking up a hill from 100 m to 300 m. At some point you must be at 200 m.","A very useful special case: if f is continuous on [a, b], and f(a) and f(b) have opposite signs, then the equation f(x) = 0 has a root between a and b. For f(x) = x³ − x − 1, f(1) = −1 and f(2) = 5, so there is a root in (1, 2). It is x = 1.3247, found by bisection.","The bisection method uses this idea. Take the middle point of the interval, and find the sign of f there. Keep the half in which the sign changes. Repeat. Each step halves the interval, so the root is found as accurately as you like.","Two warnings. The function must be continuous on the whole interval. A function with a jump, such as −1 for negative x and 1 for x at least 0, goes from −1 to 1 without ever taking the value 0. And the theorem only proves that a root exists. It does not give the value, nor the number of roots in the interval."],"e":"Show that cos x = x has a root between 0 and 1. Let f(x) = cos x − x. f(0) = 1 is positive, and f(1) = 0.54 − 1 = −0.46 is negative. By the intermediate value theorem there is a root in (0, 1). It is about 0.7391."}};
